Chapter: ListOobWrite
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 An index past the end of a list must trap on a WRITE too, and this is the
 sharpest of the indexing guards: the defect it closes corrupted a
 neighbouring allocation rather than merely disclosing one.

 Measured on the depot seed with a marker list `[999, 999, 999]` allocated
 immediately after a three-element target, `list-set-at t 5 424242` left the
 marker reading `424242 999 999`. Exit 0, no diagnostic, and the damage
 belongs to whatever else happens to hold that list.

Section: Entry

  write-past : List Integer, List Integer -> Integer
  write-past (t) (marker) =
    let corrupted = list-set-at t (list-length t + 2) 424242
    in list-at marker 0

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("marker0=" & show (write-past [1, 2, 3] [999, 999, 999]))
  end

